  <h2>Boundaries enforced in code</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>The activity itself provides the reason to continue; the system does not add streaks, daily targets or withheld rewards.</li>
    <li>The scheduler responds to approved activities and their deadlines, rather than absence or time-spent metrics.</li>
    <li>
      The review gate makes the profile available only to the models that write and run an activity.
      User-facing displays, printed sheets and panel pages receive reviewed activity content.
    </li>
    <li>The vision path processes returned sheets. Its schema carries marks or blank, rather than faces, identity, emotion, attention or biometric data.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`}>Why each boundary exists, and what it costs</a
    > · <a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/docs/prompts`}>every prompt, whole, as the model receives it</a>
  </p>
  <h2>Between activities</h2>

  <h3>Pictures</h3>
  <p>Displays can show images generated from themes chosen by the parent. The panel provides theme editing, hours and frequency for picture changes, and an archive of generated pictures. Pictures have their own display job: a dedicated screen can show them while another follows an activity.</p>

  <h3>Reminders</h3>
  <p>The parent writes a reminder as a sentence with a time and optional days, such as "On Tuesdays at 5 pm, pack the swimming bag." The house shows it on displays assigned to reminders. A button press dismisses it; otherwise it disappears at the end of its window, normally after thirty minutes. The press is not retained as evidence that the task was done.</p>

  <p>These functions also work without an activity running. On a display with several jobs, reminders take precedence over activity instructions, which take precedence over pictures. When the higher-priority content ends, the content underneath becomes visible again.</p>
